Everything here except this file is `git archive 96f7b297326e` of upstream, unmodified:
  git -C <LuaJIT clone> archive 96f7b297326e9fd454b7186c6858b2773f94a0dc | tar -x -C 3rdparty/luajit

How it is built: with its own Makefile, never from a hand-written source list.
cmake/luajit-make.sh copies this directory into the build directory and runs
  make -C src BUILDMODE=static [CROSS=<prefix>] [TARGET_SYS=<sys>] HOST_CC=<cc> XCFLAGS=... libluajit.a
there; cmake/luajit.cmake (the `luajit` target) calls it.
The build defines LUAJIT_ENABLE_CHECKHOOK (CMake option D2_LUAJIT_CHECKHOOK, default ON):
compiled traces then check the hook mask, which the host's time budget depends on.

LuaJIT's build compiles and runs host tools (minilua, buildvm) on the build machine, and
buildvm must have the target's pointer size. A 64-bit target builds anywhere with a native
cc; a 32-bit target needs a 32-bit host compiler ("gcc -m32" on x86 Linux). A Mac has none,
so for the i686-w64-mingw32 target the script runs the same make in a linux/amd64 container
(debian:trixie pinned by digest, gcc-multilib and Debian's i686 mingw-w64), and the library
stays in the build directory. LUAJIT_HOST_CC overrides the host compiler, LUAJIT_DOCKER=1
forces the container.
